WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.140
Your brain is about 2 percent of your weight, but it uses about 20 percent of your

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.320
resting energy. Who delivers fuel to the far end of an axon?

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.393
Start with the cost.

4
00:00:15.373 --> 00:00:20.133
After every impulse, the sodium-potassium pump puts sodium and potassium back.

5
00:00:21.013 --> 00:00:22.473
Each cycle spends one ATP.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:28.457
In rodent grey matter, most of the energy used for signalling goes to these pumps.

7
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.280
Now the distance. A motor axon can run from your spinal cord to your foot, about a

8
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.220
metre.

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.040
The cell body is full of mitochondria.

10
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.800
Could it just make ATP and let it diffuse down the axon?

11
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:52.153
Over a micrometre, ATP spreads in milliseconds.

12
00:00:53.273 --> 00:00:56.093
Over a millimetre, it takes about half an hour to an hour.

13
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.927
Over a metre, it would take 50 to 100 years.

14
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:08.947
So the axon must make its ATP locally, in its own mitochondria and by its own

15
00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:09.947
glycolysis.

16
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.300
Then the cover. Along a myelinated stretch in the brain and spinal cord, myelin

17
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.980
covers about 98 to 99 percent.

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.400
The bare gaps between segments are the nodes.

19
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.560
The axon meets the fluid outside mainly there.

20
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.160
Myelin is not a seal, but it limits the axon's access to glucose.

21
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:35.913
Along each stretch, the cell touching the axon is the cell that made its myelin.

22
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:41.713
Each segment is a sheet of one glial cell's membrane, wound around the axon.

23
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.913
One oligodendrocyte makes segments on many axons.

24
00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.833
A myelinating Schwann cell makes just one.

25
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:51.843
That is the one idea of this lesson.

26
00:01:52.743 --> 00:01:54.583
Whoever wraps the axon helps feed it.

27
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:01.767
An oligodendrocyte that has made its myelin can live on glycolysis.

28
00:02:02.647 --> 00:02:06.127
The evidence fits it passing lactate or pyruvate to the axon.

29
00:02:06.947 --> 00:02:13.027
When it loses MCT1, the transporter thought to carry that fuel out, axons degenerate

30
00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:14.027
as mice age.

31
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.980
The rule has limits.

32
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.700
The evidence is clearest for oligodendrocytes and for long-term upkeep.

33
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.480
The experiments do not measure each impulse, and do not rule out help there.

34
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.040
And it is help, not the only supply.

35
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.683
So keep three facts together.

36
00:02:33.563 --> 00:02:34.583
The pumps keep spending.

37
00:02:35.423 --> 00:02:36.823
The cell body is far away.

38
00:02:37.843 --> 00:02:40.323
Myelin covers almost all of a myelinated stretch.

39
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:42.723
The wrapping cell is placed to help.
